{"item_id": "fresh_N356982", "reference_source": "ruling:N356982", "product": "The merchandise under consideration is referred to as Saturnia stone. This analysis has been completed. Saturnia is a charcoal gray to black stone mottled with patches of brown and white flecks. From the information you provided, it will measure between 115 to 122 inches in length, between 65 to 75 inches in width, and either 0.75 inches or 1.5 inches in thickness. After importation, it will be cut to size and shape for such applications as kitchen countertops, bathroom surfaces, and surfaces for office furniture. The top of the stone has been polished and the sides are simply cut or sawn.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N356982, gold code 6802.99.0090:\nhas been completed. Saturnia is a charcoal gray to black stone mottled with patches of brown and white flecks. From the information you provided, it will measure between 115 to 122 inches in length, between 65 to 75 inches in width, and either 0.75 inches or 1.5 inches in thickness. After importation, it will be cut to size and shape for such applications as kitchen countertops, bathroom surfaces, and surfaces for office furniture. Laboratory analysis has determined that Saturnia is a non-calcareous metamorphic rock. The top of the stone has been polished and the sides are simply cut or sawn. The applicable subheading for the Saturnia stone will be 6802.99.0090, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Worked monumental or building stone (except slate) and articles thereof, other than goods of heading 6801…: Other: Other stone: Other: Other.” The general rate of duty will be 6.5 percent ad valorem. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Nicole Sullivan at nicole.d.sullivan@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "6802.99.00.90", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: polished (surface-worked) stone slab is worked monumental or building stone of heading 6802, not heading 2515/2516 (chapter 25 covers stone merely cut/sawn) and not 6801 setts/curbs", "GRI 6: under 6802.9 (other) the subheading turns on the stone type: marble/travertine (6802.91), calcareous (6802.92), granite (6802.93), other stone (6802.99); assumed lab identified a non-granite stone (e.g., gabbro), giving 6802.99.00", "GRI 6: statistical suffix .90 'Other' (not monuments/bases/markers)"], "rationale": "A slab with a polished top face and sawn edges is worked building stone of heading 6802, classified under 6802.9x by stone type since it is not in the 6802.2x cut-to-size group. N353775 (same template, White Spring stone, identical dimensions) and N358799 show CBP relies on laboratory identification to choose between granite (6802.93.00.10) and other stone (6802.99.00.90). The lab result for Saturnia is not stated; dark black stones sold as granite are frequently gabbro, so 6802.99.00.90 is the best guess, but 6802.93.00.10 applies if the lab found granite.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6802.93.00.10", "reason": "applies only if CBP lab identifies the stone as granite (as with Branco Fortaleza in N358799); lab result not given in description"}, {"code": "6802.91", "reason": "not marble, travertine or alabaster on the description"}, {"code": "2516.12", "reason": "top face is polished, so it is worked beyond simple sawing; chapter 25 excluded"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N359194", "reference_source": "ruling:N359194", "product": "The merchandise under consideration is a fine metallic powder, identified as HMP-SAC-38-25A Solder Powder. The stated components are tin (CAS no. 7440-31-5), silver Ag powder (CAS no. 7740-22-4), and copper metal (CAS no. 7440-50-8).", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N359194, gold code 7106.10.0000:\n alloys of Chapter 71. As such, the solder powder is precluded from classification in heading 8007, HTSUS. To determine the correct subheading within Chapter 71, we look to the Chapter’s Subheading Notes. Subheading Note 1 states that for the purposes of subheading 7106.10, the expressions “powder” and “in powder form” mean products of which 90 percent or more by weight passes through a sieve having a mesh aperture of 0.5 mm. Because CBP laboratory evaluation confirmed that more than 90 percent of the sample satisfies this sieve requirement, the product meets the legal definition of a powder. The applicable subheading for the HMP-SAC-38-25A Solder Powder will be 7106.10.0000, HTSUS, which provides for “Silver (including silver plated with gold or platinum), unwrought or in semi-manufactured forms, or in powder form: Powders.” The general rate of duty will be Free. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Yani De Leon at yaniri.deleon@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8007.00.32.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: a tin-based alloy powder without flux is not a solder preparation of 3810 (no flux/auxiliary materials) and not flux-coated/cored wire or rods of 8311", "Chapter 71 note 5 considered: an alloy is a precious-metal alloy only if silver is 2 percent or more by weight; silver share not given, assumed below threshold / tin alloy classified in chapter 80 (base-metal alloy of Section XV, section XV note 5: tin predominates)", "GRI 1: heading 8007 'Other articles of tin' covers tin powders and flakes", "GRI 6: 8007.00.32.00 Powders and flakes"], "rationale": "The product is a fine metal powder of tin alloyed with silver and copper, with no flux, so it is not a solder preparation of 3810. Heading 8007 covers tin powders and flakes (8007.00.32.00), and NY N357709 classified tin-lead solder powder there. However, chapter 71 note 5 treats an alloy as a silver alloy if silver is 2 percent or more by weight, and many SAC solders contain about 3 percent silver, which would move the powder to 7106.10; the silver percentage is not given, so the answer depends on that fact.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "7106.10.00.00", "reason": "silver powder/silver alloy powder would apply only if silver is 2 percent or more by weight under chapter 71 note 5; silver content not stated"}, {"code": "3810.10.00.00", "reason": "solder pastes/powders with flux or other materials; description lists only metals"}, {"code": "8001.20.00.00", "reason": "unwrought tin alloy in ingot/bar form; powders are specifically in 8007.00.32"}, {"code": "8311.90.00.00", "reason": "8311 covers wire, rods, electrodes, not loose powder"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N362359", "reference_source": "ruling:N362359", "product": "The imported item is an unfinished facial exfoliating cloth composed of two layers of 100 percent cotton cheesecloth fabric. The two layers of fabric are treated with tannin pre-mordant, cut into 6 x 6-inch squares, and sewn together with an overlock stitch. After importation, a charcoal dye is applied, and a decorative zigzag border stitch is sewn around the edges. The finished product is sold as a facial exfoliating cloth under the Romjay™ brand. The “So Polished Facial Cloth Refill,” SKU R-LE002, is sold at retail with two cloths. The “So Polished Facial Cloth Kit,” SKU K-LE001, is sold at retail with two cloths and a magnetic hanger.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N362359, gold code 6302.91.0060:\nIn your letter dated June 15, 2026, you requested a tariff classification ruling for an exfoliating cloth. A sample of the product was provided to this office and will be retained for training purposes. The imported item is an unfinished facial exfoliating cloth composed of two layers of 100 percent cotton cheesecloth fabric. The two layers of fabric are treated with tannin pre-mordant, cut into 6 x 6-inch squares, and sewn together with an overlock stitch. After importation, a charcoal dye is applied, and a decorative zigzag border stitch is sewn around the edges. The finished product is sold as a facial exfoliating cloth under the Romjay™ brand. The “So Polished Facial Cloth Refill,” SKU R-LE002, is sold at retail with two cloths. The “So Polished Facial Cloth Kit,” SKU K-LE001, is sold at retail with two cloths and a magnetic hanger. Classification under the Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS) is made in accordance with the General Rules of Interpretation (GRI’s). GRI 2(a) provides that any reference in a heading to an article shall be taken to include a reference to that article incomplete or unfinished, provided that, as entered, the incomplete or unfinished article has the essential character of the complete or finished article. It shall also include a reference to that article complete or finished (or failing to be classified as complete or finished by virtue of this rule), entered unassembled or disassembled. The Explanatory Notes (ENs) to the Harmonized Commodity Description and Coding System represent the official interpretation of the Customs Cooperation Council on the scope of each heading; although neither binding upon the contracting parties to the Harmonized System Convention nor considered to be dispositive interpretations, they may be consulted on the proper scope of the heading. In your letter, you suggest the exfoliating cloth is classified under subheading 9616.20.0000, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Scent sprayers and similar toilet sprayers, and mounts and heads therefor; powder puffs and pads for the application of cosmetics or toilet preparations.” We disagree because they are not of the same class or kind as the exemplars listed in Explanatory Notes (ENs) 9616. Therefore, classification in subheading 9616.20.0000 is precluded. Alternatively, you suggest that the exfoliating cloth should be classified under subheading 6307.90.9889, HTSUS; however, the suggested classification is no longer valid as of midnight June 30, 2020. The current classification is 6307.90.9891, HTSUS; however, for an article to be considered a made-up article of textile under subheading 6307.90.9891, HTSUS, there cannot be a more specific heading that also provides for the classification of the article. As the article is more specifically provided for in Heading 6302, HTSUS, it will be classified therein. Lastly, you suggest classification of the exfoliating cloth under subheading 6302.90.0010, HTSUS, which is also not a valid subheading. We assume you intended to indicate 6302.91.0060, HTSUS, which provides for “Bed linen, table linen, toilet linen and kitchen linen: Other: Of cotton…Other: Other.” We agree. The duty rate will be 9.2 percent ad valorem. While a marking ruling has not been requested, we note that the submitted sample marked “Made in USA with imported fabric from China” and packaging marked “Made with Love in USA Imported fabric from China” are not legally mar", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "6302.91.00.50", "deciding_gri": "GRI 2(a)", "gri_path": ["GRI 2(a): the imported cut, layered and overlocked squares already have the size, shape and construction of the finished facial cloth; post-importation dyeing and decorative stitching do not change its identity, so it is classified as the finished article", "GRI 1: heading 6302 toilet linen covers face cloths/washcloths; chapter 63 note 1 (made up articles) satisfied by cutting to shape and sewing; section XI note 7 made up", "GRI 6: 6302.91 other toilet linen of cotton (not terry toweling, 6302.60, since cheesecloth is not terry); not of pile or tufted construction", "GRI 6: statistical suffix under Other: Towels: Other: Other (369), 6302.91.00.50"], "rationale": "The imported squares are made up articles with the essential character of the finished facial cloth, so GRI 2(a) classifies them as the finished good. Facial cloths and exfoliating face mitts are toilet linen of heading 6302 (NY N327013 placed an exfoliating facial mitt in 6302.93). Because the fabric is cotton woven cheesecloth, not terry and not pile, it falls in 6302.91.00, and as a towel-type face cloth it takes the Towels: Other: Other (369) suffix .50.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6302.60.00.30", "reason": "cheesecloth is a plain open weave, not terry toweling"}, {"code": "6302.91.00.25", "reason": "that suffix is for cotton towels of pile or tufted construction"}, {"code": "6307.90.98.91", "reason": "residual made-up articles; 6302 toilet linen is more specific for reusable face cloths"}, {"code": "5208", "reason": "article is made up (cut to shape and sewn), so not piece-goods fabric of chapter 52"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N362421", "reference_source": "ruling:N362421", "product": "Pantoprazole Sodium, imported in 20 mg, and 40 mg delayed-release tablets, belongs to a class of medications called proton pump inhibitors (PPIs). It is indicated for the treatment of erosive esophagitis, gastric acid hypersecretion, and to promote healing of tissue damage caused by gastric acid.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N362421, gold code 3004.90.9255:\nTARIFF NO.: 3004.90.9255 Ciara Horgan Northstar Healthcare UC 3300 Cork Airport Business Park Cork T12XN72 Ireland RE: The tariff classification of Pantoprazole Sodium Tablets in dosage form, from India Dear Ms. Horgan: In your letter dated June 18, 2026, you requested a tariff classification ruling. Pantoprazole Sodium, imported in 20 mg, and 40 mg delayed-release tablets, belongs to a class of medications called proton pump inhibitors (PPIs). It is indicated for the treatment of erosive esophagitis, gastric acid hypersecretion, and to promote healing of tissue damage caused by gastric acid. The applicable subheading for the Pantoprazole Sodium Tablets in dosage form will be 3004.90.9255, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Medicaments … consisting of mixed or unmixed products for therapeutic or prophylactic uses, put up in measured doses … or in forms or packings for retail sale: Other: Other: Other: Medicaments primarily affecting the digestive system: Containing pantoprazole or its salts.” The general rate of duty will be free. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This merchandise may be subject to the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act and/or The Public Health Security and Bioterrorism Preparedness and Response Act of 2002 (The Bioterrorism Act), which are administered by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). Information on the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act, as well as The Bioterrorism Act, can be obtained by calling the FDA at 1-888-463-6332, or by visiting their website at www.fda.gov. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruli", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3004.90.92.55", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 3004 covers medicaments for therapeutic use put up in measured doses; tablets are measured doses; not 3003 (bulk) nor 2933 (bulk chemical)", "GRI 6: 3004.90 other (not antibiotics, hormones, alkaloids, vitamins of 3004.10-3004.60), 3004.90.92 other", "GRI 6: statistical suffix under Medicaments primarily affecting the digestive system: Containing pantoprazole or its salts (current 2026 Rev 20 tree, 3004.90.92.55)"], "rationale": "Pantoprazole sodium delayed-release tablets are medicaments in measured doses of heading 3004, falling in 3004.90.92 as other medicaments. NY N356509 (in force) classified pantoprazole sodium tablets in 3004.90.92 under the pantoprazole statistical line. The current revision's tree lists 'Containing pantoprazole or its salts' at 3004.90.92.55 (a revision diff shows the line was .57 in the 2026 Basic edition), so .55 is used.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "2933.39", "reason": "bulk pantoprazole sodium is a chapter 29 chemical; here it is put up in dosage form"}, {"code": "3004.90.92.57", "reason": "suffix cited in N356509/N337151 under the earlier 2026 Basic tree; in the current revision .57 is glycopyrrolate and pantoprazole moved to .55"}, {"code": "3004.90.92.60", "reason": "residual digestive-system suffix; a specific pantoprazole line exists"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N362589", "reference_source": "ruling:N362589", "product": "Style LD-4330 is a men’s jacket constructed from both knit and woven components. The hood, sleeves, back yoke, and the shoulders, are constructed from 100% polyamide woven fabric. The balance of the garment is constructed from 82% polyester, and 18% elastane knit fabric. The hood and sleeves are removable and attached at the shoulders by means of zipper closures. The garment features a hood, full frontal opening secured with zipper closures, round neckline, long sleeves with thumb holes and an elasticized binding tape at the cuffs, pockets below the waist secured by zipper closures, and the garment bottom is bound with an elasticized binding tape. Neither the knit fabric nor the woven fabric exceeds 60% of the garment’s visible surface area. Moreover, neither fabric imparts the essential character of the garment.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N362589, gold code 6201.40.7511:\neeves with thumb holes and an elasticized binding tape at the cuffs, pockets below the waist secured by zipper closures, and the garment bottom is bound with an elasticized binding tape. Neither the knit fabric nor the woven fabric exceeds 60% of the garment’s visible surface area. Moreover, neither fabric imparts the essential character of the garment. Therefore, style LD-4330 will be classified in accordance with the General Rules of Interpretation (GRIs), specifically 3(c), under the heading that occurs last in numerical order in the Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS). The applicable subheading for style LD-4330 will be 6201.40.7511, HTSUS provides for Men’s or boys’ overcoats, carcoats, capes, cloaks, anoraks (including ski-jackets), windbreakers and similar articles (including padded, sleeveless jackets), other than those of heading 6203: Of man-made fibers: Anoraks (including ski-jackets), windbreakers and similar articles (including padded, sleeveless jackets): Other: Other: Other: Other: Other: Men’s. The general rate of duty will be 27.7 percent ad valorem. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Rosemarie Haywa", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "6201.40.75.11", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(c)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: the jacket is prima facie classifiable in 6101 (knit) and 6201 (woven) because it combines knit and woven components; chapter 61 and 62 notes do not resolve it", "GRI 3(a): each heading refers to only part of the materials, so they are equally specific", "GRI 3(b): per the facts, neither the knit nor the woven fabric imparts the essential character", "GRI 3(c): heading 6201 (woven) occurs last in numerical order over 6101", "GRI 6: 6201.40 of man-made fibers; anoraks, windbreakers and similar; not recreational performance outerwear (no waterproof/taped-seam facts), no down, not water resistant stated; Other: Men's (634), 6201.40.75.11"], "rationale": "The jacket has knit and woven components, neither exceeding 60 percent of visible surface and neither giving essential character, so GRI 3(b) fails and GRI 3(c) sends it to 6201, the later of 6101 and 6201. It is an anorak/windbreaker-type jacket of man-made fibers (polyamide and polyester). NY N353235 classified a men's knit/woven outer jacket of man-made fibers in 6201.40.7511, the same statistical line used here, absent water-resistance or performance-outerwear features.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6101.30", "reason": "knit heading; under GRI 3(c) the later heading 6201 prevails when neither fabric gives essential character"}, {"code": "6201.40.70.00", "reason": "water resistant line; no water resistance (AATCC 35) stated"}, {"code": "6201.40.50.11", "reason": "recreational performance outerwear requires specific features (waterproof, taped seams etc.) not described"}, {"code": "6203.33", "reason": "suit-type jacket/blazer; this is a hooded zip-front outerwear jacket"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N362701", "reference_source": "ruling:N362701", "product": "In your submission, the subject product is identified as a DIY Acrylic Painting Kit. This activity kit is intended for all ages and is imported in a condition suitable for retail sale. The kit is comprised of five acrylic paint pods in assorted colors, two paint brushes, one clear acrylic sheet, one sheet of paper with a printed image, and one small wooden stand. All of the kit’s components are intended to be used together to creatively paint and display the acrylic sheet.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N362701, gold code 3213.10.0000:\nent material in relation to the use of the article. GRI 3 (c) provides that when goods cannot be classified by reference to GRI 3 (a) or 3 (b), they are to be classified in the heading that occurs last in numerical order among those which equally merit consideration. The DIY Acrylic Painting Kit will be classified as a set for tariff classification purposes in accordance with GRI 3(b), with the essential character imparted by the paints and paint brushs (colors in sets); we believe these components are indispensable to the product’s primary function and use, which is the activity of painting. The applicable subheading for the DIY Acrylic Painting Kit will be 3213.10.0000, Harmonized tariff Scheule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for Artists’, students’ or signboard painters’ colors, modifying tints, amusement colors and the like, in tablets, tubes, jars, bottles, pans or in similar forms or packings: Colors in sets. The general rate of duty will be 6.5 percent ad valorem on the entire set. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. This merchandise may be subject to the requirements of the Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA), which are administered by the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency. Information on the TSCA can be obtained by contacting the EPA at 1200 Pennsylvania Avenue, N.W., Mail Code 70480, Washington, D.C., by telephone at (202) 554-1404, or by visiting their website at www.epa.gov The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "3213.10.00.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 3(b)", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: the components fall in different headings (3213 paints, 9603 brushes, 3920/3926 acrylic sheet, 4911 printed paper, 4420 wooden stand); no single heading covers the kit", "GRI 3(a): each heading covers only part of the set, so they are equally specific", "GRI 3(b): retail set (different articles, one activity, put up for retail sale); the acrylic paints give the essential character because they are indispensable to the painting activity", "GRI 6: 3213.10.00.00 colors in sets"], "rationale": "The kit is a GRI 3(b) retail set: different articles classifiable in different headings, put up together for one painting activity and sold at retail. CBP consistently finds the paints give essential character to such painting kits because they are indispensable to the activity, as in NY N347383 (Painting Art Kit, 3213.10.0000). The kit is therefore classified as artists' colors in sets, 3213.10.00.00.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "3213.90.00.00", "reason": "paints are put up as a set, so 3213.10 colors in sets applies"}, {"code": "9503.00.00.90", "reason": "an adult/all-ages craft painting kit is not a toy; essential character from the paints"}, {"code": "9603.30", "reason": "brushes are subsidiary to the paints"}, {"code": "3926.90.99", "reason": "acrylic sheet is the painting surface but the paints give the kit its character"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N362750", "reference_source": "ruling:N362750", "product": "The merchandise under consideration is identified as the Solid Plenum Cat6 cable with is described as a gigabit ethernet data cable which is commonly used for structured cabling projects such as vertical, horizontal, or backbone systems. The cable is internally constructed of 23 AWG copper wire insulated with FEP. Two color matched conductors are twisted together to form a pair, and four pairs are assembled to form a core. The core is jacked with a low smoke flame retardant PVC. The cables are imported on reels to be cut to the desired length by the end user and are not terminated with any connectors. The rated voltage of the cables is stated to be 48 volts. In use, the cables transmit data and other information within various installation scenarios.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N362750, gold code 8544.49.3080:\nonductors are twisted together to form a pair, and four pairs are assembled to form a core. The core is jacked with a low smoke flame retardant PVC. The cables are imported on reels to be cut to the desired length by the end user and are not terminated with any connectors. The rated voltage of the cables is stated to be 48 volts. In use, the cables transmit data and other information within various installation scenarios. In your request, you suggest that the correct classification for the subject cables is under 8544.49.3080, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS). We agree. The applicable subheading for the Solid Plenum Cat6 cable , will be 8544.49.3080, HTSUS, which provides for “Insulated (including enameled or anodized) wire, cable (including coaxial cable) and other insulated electric conductors, whether or not fitted with connectors; optical fiber cables, made up of individually sheathed fibers, whether or not assembled with electric conductors or fitted with connectors: Other electrical conductors, for a voltage not exceeding 1,000V: Other: Other: Of copper: Other.” The general rate of duty will be 5.3 percent ad valorem. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruli", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8544.49.10.00", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8544 insulated electric conductors; not coaxial (8544.20), not fitted with connectors (so not 8544.42)", "GRI 6: 8544.49 other electric conductors for a voltage not exceeding 1,000 V, not fitted with connectors", "GRI 6: rated voltage 48 V falls under 'For a voltage not exceeding 80 V'; the cable is of a kind used for telecommunications (data transmission), 8544.49.10.00"], "rationale": "The Cat6 cable is an insulated copper electric conductor of heading 8544, rated under 1,000 V and not fitted with connectors, so it falls in 8544.49. CBP rulings on the same kind of LAN cable (e.g., NY N357575) used 8544.49.3080 because those cables were rated 300 V; here the stated rated voltage is 48 V, which places the cable in the 'not exceeding 80 V' group. Because it carries data in network cabling, it is of a kind used for telecommunications, 8544.49.10.00.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8544.49.30.80", "reason": "for conductors rated over 80 V up to 1,000 V (N357575 LAN cable was rated 300 V); this cable is rated 48 V"}, {"code": "8544.49.20.00", "reason": "'Other' at 80 V or less; this cable is of a kind used for telecommunications"}, {"code": "8544.42", "reason": "cable is not fitted with connectors"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N362809", "reference_source": "ruling:N362809", "product": "The item, described as a “Woven Quilted Moving Blanket,” is a furniture moving blanket intended to protect furniture from damage while moving. The blanket consists of three layers quilted together: a middle nonwoven felt batting layer of 85 percent polyester and 15 percent cotton is sandwiched between two layers of 100 percent polyester, dyed woven fabric. The rectangular shaped blanket measures 72 inches in length by 80 inches in width and is bound along the edges with a 2-inch wide, 100 percent polyester woven fabric. You state that the binding fabric is typically plain but can optionally be silk-screen printed with a company’s logo. You also state that the moving blanket is not machine washable. You state the manufacturing operations for the moving blanket are as follows: CHINA: • Cotton is grown and harvested. • Polyester yarn is extruded and spooled. • Outer shell (top and bottom): 100 percent polyester woven fabric is woven. • Middle layer: Nonwoven felt batting of 85 percent polyester and 15 percent cotton is produced. • 100 percent polyester woven binding fabric is produced. • Fabrics are dyed and may optionally be printed. • Polyester sewing thread, label, hangtag, polyethylene (PE) bags and packaging are produced. • Top fabric, nonwoven batting, and bottom fabric are cut and quilted together. • Binding fabric is sewn around the edges to finish the blanket. • The", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N362809, gold code 6307.90.9891:\nore textile assembled in layers, whether or not padded.” As the moving blanket is sewn by quilting and bound along the edges, it is considered a made up textile product. Thus, heading 5811 is inapplicable as the Explanatory Notes to heading 5811, Exclusion (c), specifically excludes finished, made-up articles. Lastly, you suggested that the moving blanket should be classified under subheading 6307.90.9891, HTSUS, which provides for “Other made up articles, including dress patterns: Other: Other: Other: Other: Other.” We agree. The rate of duty will be 7 percent ad valorem. COUNTRY OF ORIGIN - LAW AND ANALYSIS: Section 334 of the Uruguay Round Agreements Act (codified at 19 U.S.C. 3592), enacted on December 8, 1994, provided rules of origin for textiles and apparel entered, or withdrawn from warehouse for consumption, on and after July 1, 1996. Section 102.21, Customs Regulations (19 C.F.R. 102.21), published September 5, 1995, in the Federal Register, implements Section 334 (60 FR 46188). Section 334 of the URAA was amended by section 405 of the Trade and Development Act of 2000, enacted on May 18, 2000, and accordingly, section 102.21 was amended (68 Fed. Reg. 8711). Thus, the country of origin of a textile or apparel product shall be determined by the sequential application of the general rules set forth in paragraphs (c)(1) through (5) of Section 102.21. Paragraph (c)(1) states, “The country of origin of a textile or apparel product is the single country, territory, or insular possession in which the good was wholly obtained or produced.” As the quilted moving blanket was wholly obtained or produced in a single country, that is China, the country of origin is conferred in China. HOLDING: The moving blanket is classified under 6307.90.9891, HTSUS, and the country of origin is China. The tariffs and additional duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Kristine Dodge at kristine.dodge@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "6307.90.98.91", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: a made-up textile article (chapter 63 note 1, section XI note 7); not a bed/traveling blanket of 6301 because it is a protective moving pad, not for warmth", "GRI 1: not 5811 (quilted textile products in the piece) since it is cut and bound to a finished article; not 9404 bedding", "GRI 1: heading 6307 other made up articles", "GRI 6: 6307.90 other, 6307.90.98 other, statistical suffix .91 other"], "rationale": "The quilted moving blanket is a made-up textile article whose function is to protect furniture, not to provide warmth, so it is excluded from blankets of heading 6301 and falls in the residual heading 6307. NY N357875 (in force), a country-of-origin ruling on a furniture moving blanket, states the blanket is classified in 6307.90.9891. That statistical line is a current leaf.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "6301.40.00.20", "reason": "heading 6301 covers blankets for warmth (bed/traveling rugs); moving blankets are protective pads"}, {"code": "5811.00", "reason": "quilted fabric in the piece; this is a finished, bound article"}, {"code": "9404.90", "reason": "not bedding or an article of bedding"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N362823", "reference_source": "ruling:N362823", "product": "Product information was submitted with your request. The items under consideration are five models of floating hull protectors. They are described as floating structures designed to reduce marine growth on submerged hull surfaces by limiting water contact and movement. During use, the hull protectors are positioned on the water’s surface and secured to a marina berth with mooring lines. A vessel is then guided onto the floating protector’s upper surface where it will remain stationary. Once the vessel is positioned, standing water is routed away from the hull while outer bumpers stop additional water from entering the area. Based on the submitted information, the floating hull protectors primarily consist of reinforced, marine-grade polyvinyl chloride (PVC) materials, buoyant closed-cell foam, and laminated low-density polyethylene (LDPE) bubble material. The floating hull protectors will be imported fully assembled and ready to use in five sizes: extra small, small, medium, large, and extra large. Despite different sizes, you clarify that all models are identical in construction, materials, and principal use. You assert that the floating hull protectors will neither be attached to nor form a part of a vessel. Additionally, they will not function as a dock, pier, or fixed marine structure.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N362823, gold code 8907.90.0090:\nty polyethylene (LDPE) bubble material. The floating hull protectors will be imported fully assembled and ready to use in five sizes: extra small, small, medium, large, and extra large. Despite different sizes, you clarify that all models are identical in construction, materials, and principal use. You assert that the floating hull protectors will neither be attached to nor form a part of a vessel. Additionally, they will not function as a dock, pier, or fixed marine structure. You propose classification in subheading 8907.90, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS). We agree. The applicable subheading for the floating hull protectors will be 8907.90.0090, HTSUS, which provides for “Other floating structures (for example, rafts, tanks, cofferdams, landing-stages, buoys and beacons): Other: Other.” The general rate of duty will be Free. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (C.F.R.), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 C.F.R. 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of the Customs and Border Protection Regulations (19 C.F.R. 177). A copy of the ruling or the control number indicated above should be provided with the entry documents filed at the time this merchandise is imported. If you have any questions regarding the ruling, please contact National Import Specialist Paul Taylor at paul.m.taylor@cbp.dhs.gov. Sincerely, (for) James P. Forkan Director National Commodity Specialist Division ", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "8907.90.00.90", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: heading 8907 covers 'other floating structures' (rafts, tanks, cofferdams, landing-stages, buoys and beacons); the hull protector is a non-navigable floating structure moored at a berth", "Section XVII note 2 / chapter 89: the article is not a part or accessory of a vessel (not attached to or forming part of a vessel), so no parts provision applies", "GRI 1: a named heading for floating structures prevails over the residual plastics heading 3926 and 6307", "GRI 6: 8907.90.00 other (not inflatable raft), statistical suffix .90 other (not buoys or tanks)"], "rationale": "The hull protector is a buoyant, non-navigable floating structure moored at a berth, which fits heading 8907 'other floating structures' under GRI 1. It is not a part of a vessel and not a dock of 8905. NY I87186 classified a modular floating system in 8907.90.0090, and the same statistical 'Other' line (not buoys or tanks) is used here.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "8907.10.00.00", "reason": "not an inflatable raft; buoyancy from closed-cell foam"}, {"code": "3926.90.99", "reason": "residual articles of plastics; heading 8907 specifically describes floating structures"}, {"code": "8905.90", "reason": "not a floating dock or vessel whose navigability is subsidiary to a main function; it is a simple moored floating platform, and the importer states it is not a dock"}]}}
{"item_id": "fresh_N362874", "reference_source": "ruling:N362874", "product": "The subject merchandise consists of hexane-1,3,6-tricarbonotrile (CAS No. 1772-25-4), in liquid form, with a purity of 99.5 percent or greater. The product is primarily used as an additive in lithium-ion battery electrolytes.", "cbp_reference": "CBP ruling N362874, gold code 2926.90.5050:\n Classification TARIFF NO.: 2926.90.5050 Eunyoung Shim Shinhan Customs Service 704, Nonhyeon-ro Seoul 06052 South Korea RE: The tariff classification of hexane-1,3,6-tricarbonotrile (CAS No. 1772-25-4) in bulk form, from China Dear Eunyoung Shim: In your letter dated July 6, 2026, you requested a tariff classification ruling on behalf of your client, Dongwha Electrolyte. The subject merchandise consists of hexane-1,3,6-tricarbonotrile (CAS No. 1772-25-4), in liquid form, with a purity of 99.5 percent or greater. The product is primarily used as an additive in lithium-ion battery electrolytes. The applicable subheading for hexane-1,3,6-tricarbonotrile, in bulk form, will be 2926.90.5050, Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS), which provides for “Nitrile-function compounds: Other: Other: Other.” The general rate of duty will be free. The duties cited above are current as of this ruling’s issuance. Duty rates are provided for your convenience and are subject to change. The text of the most recent HTSUS and the accompanying duty rates are provided at https://hts.usitc.gov/. This ruling does not address the applicability of any additional duties, taxes, fees, exactions and/or other charges, which may apply to the goods discussed herein. This includes, but is not limited to, tariffs and other duties as provided for in Subchapter III to Chapter 99, HTSUS. Thus, for example, in addition to the classification stated above, the merchandise covered by this ruling may also need to be reported with either the Chapter 99 provision under which an additional tariff applies or one of the Chapter 99 provisions covering exceptions to such tariffs. For further information to assist with the importation process, please refer to the frequently updated Cargo Systems Messaging Service (CSMS) messages at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/automated/cargo-systems-messaging-service and the Trade Remedies page at https://www.cbp.gov/trade/programs-administration/trade-remedies. This merchandise may be subject to the requirements of the Toxic Substances Control Act (TSCA), which are administered by the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA). Information on the TSCA can be obtained by contacting the EPA at Document Control Office (7407M), Office of Pollution Prevention and Toxics (OPPT), Environmental Protection Agency, 1200 Pennsylvania Avenue, N.W., Washington, D.C., 20460, by calling the Toxic Substances Control Act Hotline at 800-471-7127, by e-mailing to tsca-hotline@epa.gov, or by visiting their website at www.epa.gov. The holding set forth above applies only to the specific factual situation and merchandise description as identified in the ruling request. This position is clearly set forth in Title 19, Code of Federal Regulations (CFR), Section 177.9(b)(1). This section states that a ruling letter is issued on the assumption that all of the information furnished in the ruling letter, whether directly, by reference, or by implication, is accurate and complete in every material respect. In the event that the facts are modified in any way, or if the goods do not conform to these facts at time of importation, you should bring this to the attention of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) and submit a request for a new ruling in accordance with 19 CFR 177.2. Additionally, we note that the material facts described in the foregoing ruling may be subject to periodic verification by CBP. This ruling is being issued under the provisions of Part 177 of t", "assistant_answer": {"hts10": "2926.90.50.50", "deciding_gri": "GRI 1", "gri_path": ["GRI 1: a separate chemically defined organic compound (chapter 29 note 1(a)) with nitrile functional groups falls in heading 2926 nitrile-function compounds", "GRI 6: 2926.90 other (not acrylonitrile, 1-cyanoguanidine, fenproporex, isophthalonitrile etc.)", "GRI 6: aliphatic compound, not aromatic, so not the 2926.90.01-48 aromatic lines (and not a section VI additional U.S. note 3 product); 2926.90.50 other; suffix .50 other (not malononitrile)"], "rationale": "Hexane-1,3,6-tricarbonitrile is a separate chemically defined aliphatic organic compound whose characteristic groups are nitriles, so it falls in heading 2926 under chapter 29 note 1(a). Its battery-additive use does not remove it from chapter 29 since it is imported pure and unmixed. Among the 2926.90 lines it is non-aromatic, so it takes 2926.90.50, and the residual suffix .50, consistent with NY E88551 placing an aliphatic nitrile in 2926.90.5050.", "rejected_alternatives": [{"code": "2926.90.48.01", "reason": "aromatic nitrile lines; hexanetricarbonitrile is aliphatic"}, {"code": "3824.99", "reason": "product is a single chemically defined compound of high purity, not a mixture/prepared electrolyte"}, {"code": "2926.90.50.10", "reason": "applies only to malononitrile"}]}}
